course: dictionary
lesson: 8284
title: Ripe: ready and full
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. «The apples on the tree are ripe and sweet.» Here ripe means ready to eat or pick: the fruit is soft, sweet and has its full colour.
explain: Read the other words to check. «The time is ripe for the new plan.» There is no fruit here. Ripe means ready for something, so the plan is ready to start.
explain: Then there is «a ripe old age». Ripe means a very old age here. Swap in your meaning, "a very old age", and the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right one.
fact: Ripe is an adjective, so it describes a noun. Its forms are ripe, riper and ripest.
fact: «Ripe» fruit or grain is ready to eat or pick.
fact: «Ripe» can also mean ready for something, as in «The time is ripe».
fact: Fruit ripens as starch turns into sugar, so it becomes sweet.
fact: Unripe fruit is often green, hard and sour; ripe fruit is soft, sweet and bright.
exercise: «The apples on the tree are ripe and sweet.» What does ripe mean here? | Very old | *Ready to eat | Green and hard | Cold and wet
exercise: «The time is ripe for the new plan.» What does ripe mean here? | Bright orange | *Ready for something | Rotten and smelly | Full of seeds
exercise: Unripe fruit is often green, hard and sour. What word tells you unripe fruit is not sweet? | *sour | green | hard | fruit
test: Jayden reads «a ripe old age». What does ripe mean? | Ready to eat | Ready for the new plan | Juicy and sweet | *Very old
test: «Ripe» fruit turns soft, sweet and bright. What is the opposite of ripe fruit? | *Green and hard | Soft and sweet | Golden and dry | Bright orange
exam: In the story «ripe golden wheat», what does ripe mean? | *Full grown and ready to harvest | Rotten and smelly | Cold and wet | Unripe and green
